{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"Django comes with an object-relational mapper (ORM) in which you describe your database layout in Python code.","source_span":"it comes with an `object-relational mapper`_ in which you describe your database layout in Python code"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"The example Reporter model defines a full_name field as a CharField with max_length=70.","source_span":"full_name = models.CharField(max_length=70)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"The example Article model has fields pub_date (DateField), headline (CharField, max_length=200), content (TextField), and reporter (ForeignKey to Reporter with on_delete=models.CASCADE).","source_span":"pub_date = models.DateField()\n        headline = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n        content = models.TextField()\n        reporter = models.ForeignKey(Reporter, on_delete=models.CASCADE)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"The makemigrations command looks at all available models and creates migrations for whichever tables don't already exist.","source_span":"The :djadmin:`makemigrations` command looks at all your available models and creates migrations for whichever tables don't already exist."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"The migrate command runs the migrations and creates tables in the database.","source_span":"migrate` runs the migrations and creates tables in your database"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"Django provides a free, rich Python API to access data that is created on the fly, requiring no code generation.","source_span":"you've got a free, and rich, :doc:`Python API </topics/db/queries>` to access your data. The API is created on the fly, no code generation necessary"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"The only step required to get an administrative interface for a model is to register the model in the admin site, e.g. admin.site.register(models.Article).","source_span":"The only step required is to register your model in the admin site"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"Django encourages beautiful URL design and does not put cruft like .php or .asp into URLs.","source_span":"Django encourages beautiful URL design and doesn't put any cruft in URLs, like ``.php`` or ``.asp``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","statement":"A URLconf is a Python module that contains a mapping between URL patterns and Python callback functions (views).","source_span":"it contains a mapping between URL patterns and Python callback functions"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What does Django's makemigrations command do?","answer":"It looks at all available models and creates migrations for whichever tables don't already exist."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What does the Django migrate command do?","answer":"It runs the migrations and creates the corresponding tables in the database."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[1],"question":"In the Django overview example, what field does the Reporter model have and what type is it?","answer":"It has a full_name field, defined as a CharField with max_length=70."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the only step required to make a Django model manageable through the admin interface?","answer":"Register the model in the admin site, for example with admin.site.register(models.Article)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Does Django require any code generation to provide a database API for your models?","answer":"No, the API is created on the fly with no code generation necessary."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What kind of file extensions does Django's URL design philosophy avoid?","answer":"Django avoids putting cruft like .php or .asp extensions into URLs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overview","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What fields make up the example Article model in the Django overview, including the relation to Reporter?","answer":"pub_date (DateField), headline (CharField, max_length=200), content (TextField), and reporter, a ForeignKey to Reporter with on_delete=models.CASCADE."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"The reusable-apps tutorial begins where Tutorial 8 left off and turns the web-poll app into a standalone Python package.","source_span":"This advanced tutorial begins where :doc:`Tutorial 8 </intro/tutorial08>` left off. We'll be turning our web-poll into a standalone Python package"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"For a directory like polls to form a Python package, it must contain a special file __init__.py, even if that file is empty.","source_span":"it must contain a special file ``__init__.py``, even if this file is empty."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"The tutorial uses setuptools, the recommended packaging tool (merged with the distribute fork), to build the package.","source_span":"we're going to use :pypi:`setuptools` to build our package. It's the recommended packaging tool (merged with the ``distribute`` fork)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"The tutorial uses pip to install and uninstall the package.","source_span":"We'll also be using :pypi:`pip` to install and uninstall it."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"Django recommends a django- prefix for reusable package names and a corresponding django_ prefix for the module name, e.g. the django-ratelimit package contains the django_ratelimit module.","source_span":"We recommend using a ``django-`` prefix for package names, to identify your package as specific to Django, and a corresponding ``django_`` prefix for your module name. For example, the ``django-ratelimit`` package contains the ``django_ratelimit`` module."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"Application labels must be unique in INSTALLED_APPS, and should avoid matching Django contrib package labels such as auth, admin, or messages.","source_span":"Application labels...*must* be unique in :setting:`INSTALLED_APPS`. Avoid using the same label as any of the Django :doc:`contrib packages </ref/contrib/index>`, for example ``auth``, ``admin``, or ``messages``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","statement":"After repackaging, PollsConfig in apps.py sets default_auto_field to \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\", name to \"django_polls\", and label to \"polls\".","source_span":"class PollsConfig(AppConfig):\n           default_auto_field = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\n           name = \"django_polls\"\n           label = \"polls\""}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What package and module naming convention does Django recommend for reusable apps?","answer":"A \"django-\" prefix for the package name and a corresponding \"django_\" prefix for the module name, e.g. django-ratelimit contains the django_ratelimit module."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"Which two Python packaging tools does the Django reusable-apps tutorial use, and for what purpose?","answer":"setuptools, to build the package, and pip, to install and uninstall it."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What file must a directory like polls contain to be treated as a Python package?","answer":"A special file called __init__.py, even if it is empty."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","fact_refs":[5],"question":"After repackaging the polls app, what values are set for name, label, and default_auto_field in PollsConfig?","answer":"name = \"django_polls\", label = \"polls\", and default_auto_field = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What application labels should be avoided when naming a reusable Django app?","answer":"Labels already used by Django's own contrib packages, such as auth, admin, or messages, since app labels must be unique in INSTALLED_APPS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_reusable-apps","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What does the Django reusable-apps advanced tutorial turn the web-poll app into?","answer":"It turns the web-poll app into a standalone, installable Python package that can be reused in other projects."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"The tutorial is written for a version of Django that supports Python 3.12 and later.","source_span":"This tutorial is written for Django |version|, which supports Python 3.12 and later."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"Running \"python -m django --version\" checks whether Django is installed and shows its version.","source_span":"$ python -m django --version"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"The command \"django-admin startproject mysite djangotutorial\" creates a directory named djangotutorial containing a project called mysite.","source_span":"$ django-admin startproject mysite djangotutorial ... This will create a directory ``djangotutorial`` with a project called ``mysite`` inside."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"Project names like \"django\" (conflicts with Django itself) or \"test\" (conflicts with a built-in Python package) should be avoided.","source_span":"you should avoid using names like ``django`` (which will conflict with Django itself) or ``test`` (which conflicts with a built-in Python package)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"The command \"python manage.py runserver\" starts the Django development server.","source_span":"$ python manage.py runserver"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"By default, the Django development server starts at http://127.0.0.1:8000/.","source_span":"Starting development server at http://127.0.0.1:8000/"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"The Django development server is a lightweight, pure-Python server and should not be used in anything resembling a production environment.","source_span":"**don't** use this server in anything resembling a production environment. It's intended only for use while developing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"The startproject command generates manage.py plus a mysite package containing __init__.py, settings.py, urls.py, asgi.py, and wsgi.py.","source_span":"manage.py\n        mysite/\n            __init__.py\n            settings.py\n            urls.py\n            asgi.py\n            wsgi.py"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","statement":"The development server automatically reloads Python code for each request as needed, but some actions like adding files don't trigger a restart.","source_span":"The development server automatically reloads Python code for each request as needed...However, some actions like adding files don't trigger a restart, so you'll have to restart the server in these cases."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What command checks whether Django is installed and displays its version?","answer":"python -m django --version."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What command creates a new Django project named \"mysite\" inside a directory called \"djangotutorial\"?","answer":"django-admin startproject mysite djangotutorial."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which project names does the Django tutorial say to avoid, and why?","answer":"Avoid \"django\" (it conflicts with Django itself) and \"test\" (it conflicts with a built-in Python package)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"What command starts the Django development server, and what URL does it serve by default?","answer":"python manage.py runserver, which serves the site at http://127.0.0.1:8000/ by default."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Is the Django development server suitable for production use?","answer":"No, it is a lightweight server intended only for development, not for anything resembling a production environment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_tutorial01","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What files does the startproject command create inside the inner mysite package directory?","answer":"__init__.py, settings.py, urls.py, asgi.py, and wsgi.py."}
